I"2(<p>Imagine a coding assistant that spends a week working in an unfamiliar repository. It encounters naming conventions, architectural decisions, and the reasons earlier fixes failed. How should that experience change what it can do next week?</p>

<p>Saving the conversations makes the experience retrievable. Learning from it raises further questions: what should become internal memory, when should that memory be updated, and can the model remain able to learn after many such updates?</p>

<p>In <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rAxTxqIHh9E"><em>The Next Scaling Axis of LLM Is… Sleeping?</em></a>, bycloud connects four papers around these questions. Their shared theme is the organization of learning over time. The two “sleep” papers propose different mechanisms, so understanding where each stores information is essential.</p>

