<h2 id="the-problem-retaining-the-ability-to-learn">The problem: retaining the ability to learn</h2>

<p><strong>Can Scale Save Us From Plasticity Loss in Large Language Models?</strong>, by Hernandez-Garcia, Figliolia, and Millidge, studies <em>plasticity</em>: how efficiently a trained network can learn something new. Catastrophic forgetting concerns damage to older capabilities; plasticity loss concerns deterioration in acquiring new ones.</p>

<p>The authors train GPT-style models on repeated cycles of eight languages. Periodically, they copy a checkpoint and train it on held-out Vietnamese with a fixed budget. These probe updates are discarded. Adaptation initially improves, then deteriorates with extended pretraining.</p>

<p>Across models with 5M–314M non-embedding parameters, larger models delay this deterioration. The estimated onset scales sublinearly with size, suggesting diminishing returns from scale alone. Smaller-model experiments also find the effect under a stationary language mixture.</p>

<p>The measured result concerns these architectures and training conditions. Predictions about billion-parameter models are extrapolations, rather than direct measurements in this study. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2606.24752v1#S4">Paper, Sections IV–V</a></p>

<h2 id="one-response-make-existing-weights-writable">One response: make existing weights writable</h2>

<p><strong>In-Place Test-Time Training</strong>, by Feng and colleagues, repurposes the final projection in a transformer’s feed-forward block as adaptable <em>fast weights</em>. Other projections remain stable during the inference-time updates, and attention remains part of the architecture.</p>

<p>Updates happen in chunks. Their objective is aligned with predicting the next token, so the memory is trained to store information useful for future prediction. The contribution is a way to introduce adaptive memory within an existing transformer architecture.</p>

<p>“Drop-in” describes architectural compatibility. The Qwen3-4B experiment still uses approximately 35 billion tokens of continued training for both the baseline and the enhanced model. At 128k context length, RULER accuracy rises from 74.8% to 77.0%.</p>

<p>This supports improved use of long context under the tested protocol. It does not establish indefinite retention across independent sessions or resolution of plasticity loss. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2604.06169v1#S3">Paper, Sections 3–4 and Table 1</a></p>

<h2 id="sleep-as-computation-before-forgetting-the-context">Sleep as computation before forgetting the context</h2>

<p><strong>Do Language Models Need Sleep? Offline Recurrence for Improved Online Inference</strong>, by Lee, McLeish, Goldstein, and Fanti, asks how much computation is needed to form useful memory.</p>

<p>The model combines attention with state-space memory blocks. Before clearing an attention window, it performs repeated passes that refine its fast-weight state. That state survives cache eviction and supports later single-pass prediction.</p>

<p>Experiments cover cellular automata, graph traversal, and GSM-Infinite mathematical reasoning. Additional consolidation passes improve performance, especially on harder reasoning problems. The intervention changes the computation devoted to organizing memory, while preserving the prediction-phase constraint.</p>

<p>The extra computation still costs time: preserving wake-time prediction latency does not remove the sleep interval. And persistence beyond cache eviction is different from permanent acquisition in the pretrained parameters. Resetting the fast-weight state removes this memory. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2605.26099v3#S5">Paper, Sections 5–7</a></p>

<h2 id="sleep-as-transfer-into-slower-memory">Sleep as transfer into slower memory</h2>

<p><strong>Language Models Need Sleep: Learning to Self-Modify and Consolidate Memories</strong>, by Behrouz, Hashemi, Javanmard, and Mirrokni, proposes a hierarchy of memories updated at different frequencies.</p>

<p>Its <em>knowledge seeding</em> stage activates additional capacity and distills knowledge from faster modules into slower ones. In its sparse implementation, new low-rank experts provide space for transferred knowledge. Its <em>dreaming</em> stage learns to generate synthetic training experiences, rewarding those that improve downstream performance after adaptation.</p>

<p>In the 200-passage SQuAD experiment, the four-level system reports 46.2% accuracy when answering without the passages in context, compared with 36.2% when dreaming is removed. This provides evidence for the role of synthetic rehearsal within that setup.</p>

<p>Here, sleep involves parameter expansion, knowledge transfer, and training. It is a different operation from Lee and colleagues’ recurrent refinement of fast-weight state. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2606.03979v2#S3">Paper, Sections 3–4 and Table 3</a></p>

