<p>These form a conceptual roadmap rather than a single experimentally validated pipeline. The first diagnoses a learning failure; the others investigate mechanisms for adaptation and consolidation. Combining them would require its own evidence.</p>

<p>For the coding assistant, the design questions become concrete. Which details need exact retrieval? Which recurring patterns should influence its internal behavior? How long should that influence persist? How much processing can happen between requests?</p>

<p>A useful synthesis is that memory needs both a location and an update schedule. A system can preserve a transcript yet fail to use it well. It can adapt successfully today yet become harder to teach later. Evaluating a continuing learner therefore requires tracking the acquisition of new skills, retention of earlier ones, and the computation spent achieving both.</p>

<h2 id="appendix-technical-distinctions-behind-the-headlines">Appendix: technical distinctions behind the headlines</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Plasticity measurement.</strong> The Vietnamese probe uses the area under a validation-loss curve over adaptation steps. Lower area indicates faster adaptation. The fitted onset is an empirical relationship, rather than a universal lifetime for an LLM. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2606.24752v1#S4">Sections IV–V</a></li>
  <li><strong>In-Place TTT’s update target.</strong> The analysis uses a next-token embedding target; the implementation learns a local combination of future-token embeddings. Updates affect later chunks, and fast weights reset at document boundaries. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2604.06169v1#S3.SS1">Sections 3.1–3.2</a></li>
  <li><strong>Offline recurrence’s learning rule.</strong> During deployment, memory changes through local state updates. Training backpropagates through the recurrent consolidation process to learn how those updates support later predictions. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2605.26099v3#S5">Section 5</a></li>
  <li><strong>The ARC headline.</strong> The self-modification paper’s 80% result uses eight filtered held-out tasks and reports the fraction of independently applied dreams yielding a correct answer. It is not a full-benchmark ARC accuracy. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2606.03979v2#A2.SS3">Appendix B.3</a></li>
</ul>

